const FIX = {"terms":[{"id":"pharmacodynamics","term":"Pharmacodynamics (PD)","group":"Drug basics","def":"The study of the biochemical and physiological effects of drugs and their mechanisms of action. General rules: the drug must reach the site of action, must bind to and interact with a receptor, and does not create anything but changes the ongoing physiological system.","hook":"Spoken definition: “the study of how drugs interact with the biological system.” Its two basic concepts are affinity and efficacy.","gist":"How the drug interacts with the biological system: affinity and efficacy only.","scene":"Metoprolol reaches the heart, binds the β1 receptor and blocks it, and the heart rate falls; nothing new is created, an ongoing process is changed.","confuse":["pharmacokinetics","pharmacology"],"quote":"General rules: 1) Drug must reach the site of action 2) Drug must bind to and interact with a receptor 3) Drug do not create anything, but change the ongoing phys. system","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slide ~22; transcript 9/23","src":"both"},{"id":"drug","term":"Drug","group":"Drug basics","def":"A chemical substance that alters an ongoing physiological or pathological function by interacting with components of a biological system. Drugs initiate, inhibit or modulate a response; they do not create anything new.","hook":"Walter Straub: there is only a quantitative difference between a drug and a poison (vitamin A in carrots vs polar bear liver).","gist":"A chemical that changes an ongoing function; enhances or suppresses, creates nothing new.","scene":"Metoprolol does not create a new heartbeat; it slows the rate the heart was already producing. Vitamin A in a carrot helps night blindness and in a polar bear liver poisons: only the amount differs.","confuse":["ligand","nds"],"quote":"drugs don’t create anything new, they just change whatever is happening in the body. Either they are going to enhance the signal or perhaps suppress the signal","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slides ~29–~31; transcript 9/22","src":"both"},{"id":"receptor","term":"Receptor","group":"Drug basics","def":"Regulatory proteins involved in the transfer of chemical information carried by endogenous or exogenous mediators. Classified by the structure of the receptor protein and its transduction components: ion channels, 7-transmembrane (GPCR), 1-transmembrane (tyrosine kinases) and intracellular/nuclear receptors.","hook":"His picture: the gatekeeper that senses hormones, neurotransmitters and drugs; the message the receptor creates is the physiological response. Receptors come in two flavors, active or inactive.","gist":"The regulatory protein that senses hormones, neurotransmitters and drugs; it is active or inactive.","scene":"A protein in the heart cell membrane recognizes norepinephrine by its shape and size, changes its conformation, and the cell answers with a faster beat. The same protein also binds metoprolol.","confuse":["ligand","effector","transducer"],"quote":"Receptors are regulatory proteins involved in the transfer of chemical information carried by various endogenous/exogenous mediators","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slides ~11, ~34–~36; transcript 9/22","src":"both"},{"id":"affinity","term":"Affinity","group":"Drug–receptor binding","def":"The attraction of a ligand for the receptor: how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound. It depends on the molecular forces (bonds) the drug forms with the receptor and is measured by Kd: the smaller the Kd, the greater the affinity.","hook":"Affinity is read on the x-axis (further left = higher affinity); efficacy on the y-axis. Poll trap: “the ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor” is affinity, not efficacy.","gist":"How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed.","scene":"Phenoxybenzamine forms a covalent bond with the α1 receptor and stays bound; prazosin forms weaker bonds and comes off. On a binding curve, the drug furthest left binds 50% of receptors at the smallest dose.","confuse":["efficacy","potency","kd"],"quote":"Remember, affinity is how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound to a receptor, and the binding of a drug to the receptor is going to be depending on what type of bonds they form covalent bonds, hydrogen bonds, ion bonds.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~31, ~55; Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slides ~21, ~37; transcript 9/22; transcript 9/23","src":"both"},{"id":"efficacy","term":"Efficacy (intrinsic efficacy)","group":"Drug–receptor binding","def":"The property of a molecule that causes the receptor to change its behavior: the ability of the drug to change the receptor state, active to inactive or inactive to active. Positive efficacy activates receptors, negative efficacy shuts them down, and zero efficacy is a neutral antagonist.","hook":"Read on the y-axis as the height of the curve (Emax). Efficacy cannot be calculated directly; it is a relative measurement between ligands.","gist":"The ability to change the receptor state; positive activates, negative shuts down.","scene":"Norepinephrine binds β1 and flips it from inactive to active, loratadine binds H1 and flips it from active to inactive, and metoprolol binds β1 without changing its state.","confuse":["affinity","intrinsic-activity","potency"],"quote":"Efficacy is the ability of that drug to change the receptor behavior. It’s activity, its state from active to inactive or inactive to active, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~19, ~26; Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slide ~38; transcript 9/22; transcript 9/23","src":"both"},{"id":"kd","term":"Kd (dissociation constant)","group":"Drug–receptor binding","def":"The drug concentration that binds 50% of the available binding sites (also written Ki, Ka). From the law of mass action it equals K2/K1, the rate of dissociation over the rate of association. The smaller it is, the greater the affinity: inversely proportional.","hook":"Read on the x-axis of a binding curve; it says nothing about effect. Convert units before comparing (milli 10^-3, micro 10^-6, nano 10^-9): “the units ... about 10% of you are gonna miss it.”","gist":"The concentration binding 50% of receptors; smaller means greater affinity, says nothing of effect.","scene":"For the β1 receptor drug A has a value of 30 micromolar and drug B 100 nanomolar; after converting the units the smaller number means drug B binds half of the receptors at a lower concentration (at M1 both are 250 nanomolar: equal affinity). Convert milli, micro and nano before comparing.","confuse":["ec50","affinity","mass-action"],"quote":"So the take-home message is that the smaller the coefficient of dissociation is, the more drugs receptors are going to find together, and thus the greater the affinity is going to be.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~45–~50; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slide ~31; transcript 9/24","src":"both"},{"id":"orthosteric","term":"Orthosteric site","group":"Drug–receptor binding","def":"The receptor’s own binding pocket, where the endogenous agonist binds. Drugs that bind there (phenylephrine, prazosin, phenoxybenzamine at α1) compete for that one pocket, so only one can be bound at a time: either one or the other, not both.","hook":"Orthosteric: one or the other. Allosteric: one and the other. Spoken as “orthostatic” in the transcripts; the slide term is orthosteric.","gist":"The receptor’s own pocket where the endogenous agonist binds; one or the other.","scene":"Phenylephrine, prazosin and phenoxybenzamine all go for the same pocket on the α1 receptor, the one norepinephrine uses, so only one of them can be in it at a time.","confuse":["allosteric"],"quote":"How we define orthostatic. So they compete for the same binding pocket, right? ... with orthostatic, we can have either one or the other, not both at the same time. As with allosteric, we can have one in the other","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~18–~19; transcript 9/23; transcript 9/24","src":"both"},{"id":"mass-action","term":"Law of mass action","group":"Drug–receptor binding","def":"The rate of a chemical reaction is proportional to the concentration (mass) of the reactants: the more drug or the more receptors present, the greater the chance of binding. It is the basis of Kd and of dose responsiveness.","hook":"More receptors in a tissue make the same dose of norepinephrine appear more potent (curve shifts left) while its Kd, affinity and efficacy are unchanged; what changed is the tissue.","gist":"More drug or more receptors present, greater the chance of binding.","scene":"Two hearts get the same dose of norepinephrine, one with 10 receptors and one with 100; the heart with 100 responds at a lower dose, though norepinephrine itself is unchanged.","confuse":["kd"],"quote":"The more I have either of the receptors or the more I have either the drug, the greater the capacity of binding to something is going to be, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~45; transcript 9/24","src":"both"},{"id":"cheese-effect","term":"Cheese effect (tyramine)","group":"Adrenergic drugs","def":"The hypertensive crisis that follows eating tyramine-rich fermented foods (cheese, bread, wine) while taking a non-selective MAO-A and MAO-B inhibitor such as phenelzine: the gut enzyme can no longer break down dietary tyramine. Selective MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline at low doses) do not cause it.","gist":"Fermented food plus a non-selective enzyme blocker: a blood pressure crisis.","scene":"A man taking phenelzine forgets his diet at a party, eats aged fermented food with wine, and his blood pressure soars until he passes out and an ambulance is called.","confuse":["mao-a","rebound-hypertension"],"quote":"And the reason being is because you have the inability to break down that dietary tyramine that you're consuming, and those fermented foods have high levels of tyramine, and that can cause what is called a hypertensive crisis.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6","src":"both"},{"id":"rtk","term":"Receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK, 1-TM)","group":"Receptors and signaling","def":"A 1-transmembrane receptor linked to an intracellular enzyme: an extracellular binding domain outside and intrinsic enzymatic (catalytic) activity inside. Ligand binding to the inactive monomer induces dimerization, cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domains and docking sites for signaling complexes; a tyrosine phosphatase resets it. Receptors for insulin and growth factors (cell survival, proliferation, differentiation).","hook":"Downstream: Grb2 → GEF → RAS → RAF → MEK → ERK → gene transcription; 20–25% of cancers contain a RAS mutation, so a RAS blocker is an indirect antagonist that stops cell growth.","gist":"Crosses the membrane once; binding outside, enzyme activity inside; insulin, growth factors.","scene":"Insulin binds the outer part of a protein that crosses the membrane once; two of them pair up, phosphorylate each other inside the cell, and the cascade runs through RAS, RAF, MEK and ERK to gene transcription.","confuse":["gpcr","nuclear-receptor","ion-channel"],"quote":"They have this binding pocket on the outside, and they have this enzymatic activity inside of the cell. Typically those are transcription factors like insulin growth factors.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~4–~11; Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slides ~35–~36; transcript 9/23","src":"both"}],"qs":[{"id":"L02-018","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L02","sub":"site","skill":"apply","concept":"diazepam-gaba-pam","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"GABA (γ-aminobutyric acid) is bound to its GABA-A receptor, a chloride (Cl−) ion channel, when diazepam is added. What is the result?","options":[{"t":"GABA binds better; more Cl− enters","correct":true,"why":"Diazepam is a positive allosteric modulator: it increases the affinity and/or efficacy of GABA, so more chloride enters."},{"t":"GABA is displaced; less Cl− enters","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam does not compete for the GABA site; it binds someplace else."},{"t":"Cl− leaves the neuron","correct":false,"why":"Opening the GABA channel lets chloride move from outside to inside the cell."},{"t":"Na+ enters; the neuron is more excitable","correct":false,"why":"GABA is the major inhibitory pathway; the channel conducts chloride and shuts things down."}],"teach":"GABA binds its orthosteric sites and opens the GABA-A channel; chloride enters, the membrane becomes more negative, and the person becomes less anxious and more sleepy. Diazepam, an allosteric agonist (positive allosteric modulator, PAM), binds elsewhere and increases GABA's affinity and/or efficacy, so more chloride enters faster or longer.","quote":"Benzos increase the affinity And/or efficacy of GABA For its receptor","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~21–~24; transcript 9/23"},{"id":"L02-023","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L02","sub":"class","skill":"tell","concept":"efficacy-definition","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which best describes efficacy?","options":[{"t":"Ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor","correct":false,"why":"Binding and staying bound is affinity."},{"t":"Ability of a cell to increase its number of receptors","correct":false,"why":"Receptor number is the cell's business, not a drug property."},{"t":"Ability of a drug to change the receptor behavior","correct":true,"why":"Efficacy is the drug changing the receptor state from inactive to active or active to inactive."},{"t":"Ability of a drug to be absorbed into the body","correct":false,"why":"Absorption is pharmacokinetics, not pharmacodynamics."}],"teach":"Affinity describes the association between drug and receptor (molecular forces, bonds). Efficacy (intrinsic efficacy) describes the mechanism by which the drug changes the receptor. A drug with efficacy changes the receptor state; one with zero efficacy (a neutral antagonist) does not.","quote":"Efficacy is the ability of that drug to change the receptor behavior. It's activity, its state from active to inactive or inactive to active, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; transcript 9/23"},{"id":"L10-030","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"apply","concept":"cheese-effect-crisis","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A patient taking phenelzine eats aged cheese and drinks wine at a party. Which is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Hypertensive crisis","correct":true,"why":"Non-selective MAO inhibition leaves dietary tyramine unprocessed: the “cheese effect”, a hypertensive crisis."},{"t":"Severe hypotension","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction raises blood pressure."},{"t":"Bradycardia from vagal tone","correct":false,"why":"The reaction is sympathetic and hypertensive."},{"t":"No reaction","correct":false,"why":"Fermented foods are high in tyramine, which the patient cannot break down."},{"t":"Sedation","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction is sympathetic: blood pressure climbs to a hypertensive crisis; it does not sedate."}],"teach":"A non-selective monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor blocks MAO in the gut as well as the brain, so dietary tyramine from fermented foods (cheese, bread, wine) is not broken down. The result is a hypertensive crisis, the “cheese effect”. A patient on the drug who eats cheese can pass out from the very high pressure and need emergency care.","quote":"You cannot eat anything that is fermented. Like cheese and bread and wine, all of those are off the plate, right? And the reason being is because you have the inability to break down that dietary tyramine that you're consuming, and those fermented foods have high levels of tyramine, and that can cause what is called a hypertensive crisis.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"T-affinity-3","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"TERMS","sub":"g2","skill":"term","concept":"term-affinity","tags":["term"],"source":"both","stem":"Which term is defined as: How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed?","options":[{"t":"Affinity","correct":true,"why":"This defines Affinity: How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed. Affinity is read on the x-axis (further left = higher affinity); efficacy on the y-axis. Poll trap: “the ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor” is affinity, not efficacy."},{"t":"Orthosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Orthosteric site: The receptor’s own pocket where the endogenous agonist binds; one or the other. The definition given is Affinity: How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed."},{"t":"Efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Efficacy (intrinsic efficacy): The ability to change the receptor state; positive activates, negative shuts down. The definition given is Affinity: How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed."},{"t":"Potency","correct":false,"why":"Potency (pharmacological): How much drug is needed; a combination of affinity, efficacy and the tissue. The definition given is Affinity: How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed."}],"teach":"How likely a drug binds and stays bound; depends on the bonds formed. Affinity is read on the x-axis (further left = higher affinity); efficacy on the y-axis. Poll trap: “the ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor” is affinity, not efficacy. Scene: Phenoxybenzamine forms a covalent bond with the α1 receptor and stays bound; prazosin forms weaker bonds and comes off. On a binding curve, the drug furthest left binds 50% of receptors at the smallest dose.","quote":"Remember, affinity is how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound to a receptor, and the binding of a drug to the receptor is going to be depending on what type of bonds they form covalent bonds, hydrogen bonds, ion bonds.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~31, ~55; Pharmacodynamics-Day-1-2026s.pdf slides ~21, ~37; transcript 9/22; transcript 9/23"}],"guide":"<h3 id=\"guide-1\">1. Affinity, efficacy, potency</h3>\n<h4>What it is</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Affinity</b>: how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound; set by the bonds it forms (covalent, ionic, hydrogen, van der Waals). <small>Day 2 ~55; (T) 9/23</small></li>\n<li><b>Efficacy</b>: the ability to change the receptor's state, inactive (R) or active (R*); positive (activates receptors) or negative (shuts them down). <small>Day 1 ~38; (T) 9/22, 9/23</small></li>\n<h3 id=\"guide-4\">4. Orthosteric vs allosteric</h3>\n<h4>What it is</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Orthosteric</b> drugs bind the receptor's own pocket and compete for it: one or the other (phenylephrine, prazosin, phenoxybenzamine). <small>Day 2 ~18; (T) 9/23</small></li>\n<li><b>Allosteric</b> drugs bind a second site and change the pocket, so the agonist's affinity, efficacy, both or neither changes: one and the other. <small>Day 2 ~19–~20, ~39, ~44; (T) 9/23</small></li>"};
